      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Paste a picture into a scene - and pick the model the surface deserves.\n\nRun once so the two images reach the editor, then drag the pairs. Because 'background' is connected, the annotate surface splits in TWO: the picture on the left carries the cyan 'from' ends, the wall on the right carries the orange 'to' ends, and 'points_to' comes out in the WALL's pixels - which is exactly what 'Paste Through Warp' wants. 'pin image corners' seeds a full-frame paste to deform from.\n\nTEN pairs are annotated here: the picture's four corners onto the wall's left poster, its centre, and five edge points - with the bottom edge dragged well BELOW the poster, so the target is a sagging, bowed surface and not a flat quad. The same ten pairs feed three pastes:\n  * affine - 6 parameters (rotation, scale, shear, translation), 3+ pairs. Parallel lines stay parallel, so it cannot reach the wall's perspective, let alone the sag: every pair is least-squares AVERAGED and the residual stays large (~40 px mean, ~120 px worst). Switch the combo to 'homography' and the perspective comes back - the sag still does not, which is what the combo is there to show.\n  * thin plate spline - lands every pair exactly (residual ~0) and BENDS the picture in between. This is the one for a curved poster, a flag, cloth.\n  * spline again, with 'background_paintable' - the inverse of an annotated polygon, so whatever that polygon covers stays IN FRONT of the paste (here it takes the drooping bottom away).\n\nFewer pairs than the model needs (spline 3, affine 3) is not an error: 'found' goes false, the wall comes through untouched, and the if/else stamps the picture so a passing preview cannot lie about it."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
